# NOTE: Manually transcribed by OWID from the "Youths, 10-15 yrs" columns of Table 1 (p. 7),
# which gives the labor-force participation rates as published in the census. The corrected
# 1880 rates (32.4% and 12.1%) come from the authors' PUMS tabulation, reported in the text
# on p. 9; the paper publishes no corrected figure for any other year, so the remaining rows
# of the corrected series repeat the published ones. The other Table 1 rows (married women,
# older men) are not included. Verified against the paper on 2026-09-06: all values match.
# In case of updating, upload manually.

# Learn more at:
# http://docs.owid.io/projects/etl/architecture/metadata/reference/
meta:
  origin:
    # Data product / Snapshot
    title: "Fixing the Facts: Editing of the 1880 U.S. Census of Occupations with Implications for Long-Term Labor-Force Trends and the Sociology of Official Statistics"
    description: |-
      This journal article re-examines how the 1880 US Census of occupations was edited and shows that the editorial conventions applied to it distorted long-run labor-force trends, including the labor-force participation of children and youths aged 10-15.

      Using the 1880 Public Use Microdata Sample, the authors reconstruct the youth labor-force participation rates that the published census understated, and report both the originally published rates and their corrected estimates by sex.
    date_published: "1996"

    # Citation
    producer: Carter and Sutch
    citation_full: |-
      Carter, S. B., & Sutch, R. (1996). Fixing the Facts: Editing of the 1880 U.S. Census of Occupations with Implications for Long-Term Labor-Force Trends and the Sociology of Official Statistics. Historical Methods: A Journal of Quantitative and Interdisciplinary History, 29(1), 5-24. https://doi.org/10.1080/01615440.1996.10112726

    # Files
    url_main: https://doi.org/10.1080/01615440.1996.10112726
    date_accessed: '2026-08-03'

    # License
    license:
      name: © Carter and Sutch (1996)
